<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
  <item>Here are some equations. Find the solution to each equation using what you know about exponent rules. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00004">
  <problem id="prob-00004">
    <para id="para-00010">\(9^x\cdot9^7=9^7\)</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00004">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00011">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00012">0</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00005">
  <problem id="prob-00005">
    <para id="para-00013">\(\frac{9^{12}}{9^x}=9^{12}\)</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00005">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00014">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00015">0</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00003">
  <item>Answer the following questions.</item>
</list>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00006">
  <problem id="prob-00006">
    <para id="para-00016">What is the value of \(5^0\)?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00006">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00017">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00018">For positive whole numbers, \(5^a\cdot5^b=5^{a+b}\). For this law to still hold with 0 as an exponent, \(5^0\) needs to be equal to 1 because \(5^{0+4}=5^4\) and \(1\cdot5^4=5^4\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00007">
  <problem id="prob-00007">
    <para id="para-00019">What about \(2^0\)?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00007">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00021">For positive whole numbers, \(2^a\cdot2^b=2^{a+b}\). For this law to still hold with 0 as an exponent, \(2^0\) needs to be equal to 1 because \(2^{0+4}=2^4\) and \(1\cdot2^4=2^4\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>

<!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
<section id="sect-00002">
  <title>Are you ready for more?</title>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Extend Your Thinking</title>
<!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
<exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00008">
  <problem id="prob-00008">
    <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
    
    <para id="para-00022">We know, for example, that \((2+3)+5=2+(3+5)\) and \(2\cdot\left(3\cdot 5\right)=\left(2\cdot 3\right)\cdot 5\). The grouping with parentheses does not affect the value of the expression.</para>
    
    <para id="para-00023">Is this true for exponents? That is, are the numbers \(2^{\left(3^5\right)}\) and \(\left(2^3\right)^5\) equal? If not, which is bigger?</para>
      
    </problem><solution id="sol-00008">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00024">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00025">The two numbers are not even close! Since \(3^5=243\), the number \(2^{\left(3^5\right)}\) is \(2^{243}\). On the other hand, \(\left(2^3\right)^5=2^3\cdot2^3\cdot2^3\cdot2^3\cdot2^3=2^{15}\). The number \(2^{243}\) is much larger than \(2^{15}\).</para>
    

  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction-->

<exercise class="RFM1" id="exer-00009">
  <problem id="prob-00009">
    <para id="para-00026">Which of the two would you choose as the meaning of the expression \(2^{3^5}\) written without parentheses?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00009">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00027">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00028">Typically, we define repeated exponents by working down the tower, e.g., \(2^{3^5}=2^{\left(3^5\right)}\). One reason for this is that evaluating up the tower, as in \(\left(2^3\right)^5\), can already be simplified by using algebra rules (in this case, to get \(2^{15}\)).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--End interaction--></section>
</section>

<note class="self-check" id="note-00001"><para id="para-00029">Santos and Sara are checking their work on the following problem: \(4\left(2\cdot3\cdot5\right)^0\).</para>
  <para id="para-00030">Santos says the answer is 1. Sara says the answer is 4. Who is correct, and why?</para></note>

  <section id="sect-00004">
    <title>Additional Resources</title>
    
  <section id="sect-00005"><title>Zero as an Exponent</title>
    <para id="para-00031">Why is \(2^0=1\) and \(5^0=1\), but \(2^3\) is not the same as \(5^3\)?</para>
    <para id="para-00032">Answering this question will help us understand the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">zero exponent rule</term>.</para>
    <para id="para-00033">Exponents in general represent repeated multiplication, much like multiplication represents repeated addition. Let’s use that understanding to evaluate \(2^3\) and \(5^3\):</para>
    <para id="para-00034">\(2^3=2\cdot2\cdot2=4\cdot2=8\)</para>
    <para id="para-00035">\(5^3=5\cdot5\cdot5=25\cdot5=125\)</para>
    <para id="para-00036">8 and 125 are obviously very different, but the method to get to each answer was the same. If we rewrite it as a table, we can work backward to understand how \(2^0\) and \(5^0\) are both 1.</para>
    <para id="para-00037">Using \(y=2^x\) and filling in the parts we have found results in Table 1:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00002">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
          <entry> 1 </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 3 </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 4 </entry>
          <entry> 8 </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para id="para-00038">Repeating that process for \(y=5^x\) results in Table 2:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00003">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
          <entry> 1 </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 3 </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> </entry>
          <entry> 5 </entry>
          <entry> 25 </entry>
          <entry> 125 </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para id="para-00039">Since exponents represent repeated multiplication in both tables, additional terms are found by multiplying again by the base. What if we worked backward? If you multiply to move to the right in the table, you can divide by the common ratio to work backward. Start with the 8, and work backward to fill in the table:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00004">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
          <entry> 1 </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 3 </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> \(2\div2=1\) </entry>
          <entry> \(4\div2=2\) </entry>
          <entry> \(8\div2=4\) </entry>
          <entry> 8 </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para id="para-00040">Repeat the process for Table 2:</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00005">
      <tgroup cols="5"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(x\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> 0 </entry>
          <entry> 1 </entry>
          <entry> 2 </entry>
          <entry> 3 </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>\(y\)</emphasis> </entry><entry> \(5\div5=1\) </entry>
          <entry> \(25\div5=5\) </entry>
          <entry> \(125\div5=25\) </entry>
          <entry> 125 </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    <para id="para-00041">After you have worked backward in each table, you will see that \(2^0\) and \(5^0\) both equal 1. When 1 is the exponent, your answer is the same as the common ratio, so when you divide by the common ratio, you end up with 1 as your answer every time. This is an important rule that can save a lot of time.</para>
    <note class="try" id="note-00002">
    <title>Try It: Zero as an Exponent</title>
    <exercise id="exer-00010">
      <problem id="prob-00010">
        
        <para id="para-00042">Find the value of each of the following:</para>
      
      <para id="para-00043">\(3^0\)</para>
          <para id="para-00044">\(1,246^0\)</para>
          <para id="para-00045">\(
            \left(2\cdot3\cdot7\right)^0\)</para>
        <para id="para-00046">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
      
      </problem><solution id="sol-00010">
      <para id="para-00047">Compare your answer:</para>
      
      <para id="para-00048">Here is how to use the zero exponent rule to determine the value of each:</para>
      
      <para id="para-00049">The answer to each expression is 1. If you want to work backward, it doesn’t matter how large the original number is. \(3\div3=1\) and \(1246\div1246=1\), and both expressions only contain a base and zero as exponent, so the answer is 1. For c, there is an expression inside the parentheses that you can evaluate first, but since it is still the base raised to a zero exponent, the answer will still be one.</para>
    
    </solution></exercise></note>
    </section>
  </section>
  
